WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.948
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.794 --> 00:00:08.210
REUVEN LAX: My
name is Reuven Lax.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:12.230
I'm a software engineer
on Google Cloud Platform,

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:14.560
and we're here with
our wonderful partners

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:21.110
at Scotiabank to talk about
Google Cloud Dataflow streaming

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.690
and how we autoscale
streaming pipelines.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.450
And then we will talk
about how Scotiabank

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:28.940
uses this functionality.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:33.470
So first, a little bit of
background on streaming

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.620
Dataflow pipelines and
streaming pipelines in general,

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.690
and why autoscaling
is important.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.140
So unlike traditional
batch applications

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.300
where you just run
once and then they're

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.250
done, if later, you have
more data, you run it again

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:54.890
with a larger set of
inputs and more resources,

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:58.010
streaming pipelines
are always on.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.100
You usually start a streaming
cluster, a streaming pipeline,

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.920
and just leave it
running continuously.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.120
But in most cases, the incoming
load to the streaming pipeline

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.050
varies over time.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.460
Often, these are daily
diurnal variations.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:22.650
So at midnight, you have much
lower levels of data coming in,

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.530
then at peak hours,
your volume increases

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.292
and keeps going up and
down throughout the day.

00:01:29.292 --> 00:01:30.910
And so traditionally,
what people

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:34.160
have had to do to deal
with this is to provision

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.080
their streaming pipelines
for the peak load--

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.020
figure out how
many resources do I

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.780
need to handle the
busiest time of day,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.370
make sure my
streaming pipeline has

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.200
at least that many resources--

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.670
sometimes even more because
if anything falls behind,

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:52.530
you need to process even
faster than that to catch up.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.340
And this is very expensive.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.589
You're wasting many
of your resources

00:01:58.589 --> 00:02:00.630
throughout most of the
day, and one of the things

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:04.050
we set out to solve with
Dataflow's streaming

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.160
autoscaling.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.320
So here's a quick
agenda for this talk.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.760
I'm going to start off with a
quick overview of Apache Beam

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:16.190
and Dataflow, and how we
execute streaming pipelines.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.430
I'm going to talk a bit about
what our primary signals are

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.330
for streaming autoscaling,
and how this works today

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:26.600
and in our new, upcoming
streaming engine, which

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.520
will replace the current one.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:33.590
And then finally, our
representative from Scotiabank

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:36.920
will talk about how they are
using Dataflow and autoscaling

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.390
to solve their problems.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:50.140
So here we have a visual of
essentially the Hello, World

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.150
of data processing problems.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.120
So the Hello, World of
data processing problem

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.920
that everybody likes to
start with is count words.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.930
Reading a bunch of lines and
output how many words you have.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:04.960
It's a fairly trivial
problem, but it's

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.240
the Getting Started guide
that everybody uses.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:09.820
So on the right,
this is the code

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.560
you would write
with the Apache Beam

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:16.470
API, which is the API to
Dataflow to count words.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.070
This is a streamed
pipeline, so we

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:21.490
read words for
our Pub/Sub topic,

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:25.700
split every line into
its component words.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:28.160
And here we've decided to
window it by five minutes.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.210
So our output is
every five minutes,

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:34.630
how many of each
unique words did we

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:36.460
see in that last five minutes?

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:40.570
And then finally, count
them and write the data out.

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:43.690
Now, it turns out that even
though this pipeline has

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:46.990
one, two, three, four,
five steps in it,

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:50.560
Dataflow has an optimizer that
optimizes this into a smaller

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.550
number of steps for execution.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:56.980
And what we actually end up
with is two steps separated

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.215
by, actually, the
component of this count,

00:04:00.215 --> 00:04:02.800
where we have to
shuffle data around,

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.885
so we can actually count
the words properly.

00:04:07.336 --> 00:04:08.710
And that is the
streaming shuffle

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:13.480
on the left connecting
the two steps.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.610
And it turns out that when
it comes to autoscaling,

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.950
we can usually look
at these two steps

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:22.195
independently and autoscale
each one separately.

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.630
And also, unlike
batched pipelines,

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:31.140
where you will have
steps in a sequence,

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.150
and usually one step
finishes completely,

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.733
and then when it's
done, the next step

00:04:34.733 --> 00:04:38.730
starts, in a streaming pipeline
where data is constantly

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.980
streaming through, all
steps in the pipeline

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.440
are active at the same time.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:47.222
So your autoscaling
algorithms must

00:04:47.222 --> 00:04:48.930
take into account
everything in parallel.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.050
So how do I turn on autoscaling?

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:59.940
Well, today, you turn it
on by passing in a flag

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.770
to your pipeline when
you launch it and say

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:04.920
autoscalingAlgorithm=THROUGHPUT
BASED.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.440
In the future, we
plan for autoscaling

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.270
to just be the default--

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.246
always on.

00:05:11.246 --> 00:05:12.870
But until then, this
is how you turn it

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.920
on for streaming pipelines.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.090
And you can also specify
a max num workers

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.960
flag, which will give us
a ceiling on autoscaling.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.090
So if you say max num
workers equals 1,000,

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.670
then we will never
autoscale up above 1,000,

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.940
even if it needs it to
keep up with its input.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:38.510
So how does this work
under the covers?

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:42.470
So to explain a little bit
about how our autoscaling works,

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:44.610
I have to start
off by explaining

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:48.240
a little bit about
Dataflow's execution model

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:49.230
under the covers--

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:53.010
how we take a Dataflow program
and actually schedule it

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:56.970
and run it on GCE workers.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:00.000
And the first thing
I have to explain

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.760
is that in Dataflow,
our underlying runner,

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.580
everything is done in
the context of a key.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:09.120
So all data is really
separated into key value pairs

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:12.914
that we then shuffle
around and process.

00:06:12.914 --> 00:06:14.580
If you've done a group
by key or account

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.610
by key, or in this case, account
words, then the key is obvious.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:22.590
The key is just the
word you're counting.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.420
If you were not doing
any sort of aggregation,

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:27.990
it turns out we still have a
key that we just randomly assign

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.970
to all the elements.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:33.220
We take all the keys.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:37.090
We then prefix each
one with a hash.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:40.330
So we take a hexadecimal
hash of each key

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:45.130
and put it at the beginning
of every key, which

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:46.010
does two things.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.865
First of all, it
smears out the work.

00:06:48.865 --> 00:06:50.990
So if you have a lot of
keys that are very similar,

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:52.420
they don't end up together.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.450
It tends to spread them
out across the hash space.

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:59.150
And it also gives us a nice
way of chopping this work up.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:03.190
Since every key starts
with a 16 character hash,

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:06.850
we can then just take
ranges of these hashes

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:12.070
and use that to divide up the
work into multiple charts.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:15.100
So here is an example of
Stage A. We have taken

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:18.310
all the keys, based off of
an estimate of how large we

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:19.630
think the input is.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:22.510
We have divided
the key ranges up

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:28.967
into a certain number of ranges
and started the pipeline.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:36.780
So next, we actually
start the workers

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:42.310
that will run these stages
in the streaming pipeline.

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:45.570
So here, these little blue
icons, our standard GCE icon.

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:48.630
We started up a
number of GCE workers.

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:51.870
And for each GCE worker, we take
some number of these key ranges

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.490
and assign it to that worker.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:57.570
So here, we have KRI or KRJ
are assigned to this one.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.060
KRX, KRY are assigned
to that worker.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.040
There are more
workers in between.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:05.650
Every worker has some number of
key ranges for this streaming

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:06.400
pipeline assigned.

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:16.080
Now, how does this actually
work on our system today?

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.340
And this is something
that I'll explain

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:22.680
later is changing with the
upcoming streaming engine.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:24.330
But the way the
current system works

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:29.700
is everything runs on
these GCE virtual machines.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:33.990
So every virtual machine
that has a key range assigned

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:37.230
has persistent disks
attached to it to store state

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:38.760
for that key range.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:41.400
So if you're doing a
five minute aggregation

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.627
or a one hour
windowed aggregation,

00:08:43.627 --> 00:08:45.210
you need to be able
to constantly keep

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:48.870
track, persistently, of
what your aggregations are

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:51.480
so far for this window.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:54.280
If the worker crashes
and has to restart,

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:55.930
you don't want to lose any data.

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:59.070
So currently, we use
persistent disk and a key value

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:03.180
store on top of the
persistent disk on each VM

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:04.620
to store the state.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:06.120
They'll explain later we have--

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:09.720
our new streaming engine moves
all of this into the Cloud,

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:13.995
and so all this work
moves off these workers.

00:09:13.995 --> 00:09:15.930
You also need to shuffle data.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:21.010
So Pub/Sub is a completely
unordered, unsorted source,

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:23.820
which means if I'm
counting words,

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.620
all the words might show
up on all the workers.

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:31.020
So if I want to take the
word next for instance,

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.600
and count how many nexts
I've seen over the past five

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.630
minutes, I need to make sure
all these next counts end up

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:39.420
on a unique worker so
I can do this count.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.400
So we have a streaming
shuffle system

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:44.627
that moves these records
around between the workers

00:09:44.627 --> 00:09:45.710
so we can do these counts.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:54.970
So now that I've talked a
little bit about how things work

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:59.200
under the covers, let's move
on to autoscaling for a second,

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:03.200
or for the rest of my talk.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:06.680
In order to scale a
pipeline, we need signals.

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:10.640
We need something that tells
us this pipeline doesn't have

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:11.990
enough workers attached to it.

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:13.790
We're not keeping up.

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:17.750
And also a signal that gives us
an estimate of how many workers

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.700
we need in this pipeline.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:21.050
Your pipeline is not keeping up.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:22.700
You have 15 workers.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:27.210
I estimate that with 25 workers
you'll be able to keep up.

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:31.670
So what are our primary
signals for autoscaling?

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:33.680
The first one is backlog.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.090
Is there data coming in?

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:37.940
If data is coming in faster
than we can process it,

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:41.660
then the data is going to start
piling up and form a backlog.

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:45.110
So if this backlog is high,
and especially if this backlog

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.960
keeps growing,
that's a sign that we

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:53.680
don't have enough workers
and we need to autoscale up.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:56.440
The second one is throughput.

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:58.956
How fast am I currently
processing the data?

00:10:58.956 --> 00:11:00.580
Maybe I'm currently
processing the data

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:06.640
at 10,000 QPS, 100,000
QPS, or a million QPS.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:09.490
But then we have to contrast
that with the actual input

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:10.270
rate.

00:11:10.270 --> 00:11:13.270
So the input rate is how
fast the data is actually

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:15.290
showing up at my pipeline.

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:17.520
So if I'm processing
data a million QPS,

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.220
and my input rate
is 1.2 billion QPS,

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:24.370
clearly I'm not
processing fast enough.

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:26.290
And the input rate--

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:28.120
we can always estimate
the input rate

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:31.330
by taking the throughput that
we're currently processing data

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:36.410
at, and then measuring how
fast is our backlog growing?

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:41.470
So if I'm processing data at,
say, 10 gigabytes per second,

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:42.880
but then I can
measure my backlog

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:44.255
and say, OK, but
my backlog keeps

00:11:44.255 --> 00:11:47.380
growing at 1
gigabyte per second,

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:48.820
a good estimate
of your input rate

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:53.160
is 11 gigabytes per second.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:57.040
If I was able to process that,
my backlog would stop growing.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:59.650
And finally, we look
at CPU consumption

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:04.150
to understand how much CPU
all these workers are using,

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:07.492
which is another important
signal for autoscaling.

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:14.080
So let's talk for a
second about backlog.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:17.410
So here we have Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:20.350
So a bunch of data
coming from Cloud

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:22.990
Pub/Sub into our pipeline.

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:25.390
Presumably, there is
a publisher out there

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:28.750
that is pushing data
into Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:32.590
And remember, we had two stages
in this pipeline, stage A

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:36.430
and stage B. Stage
A is a stage that's

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:37.930
pulling data from Pub/Sub.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:43.450
So the first thing
we want to measure

00:12:43.450 --> 00:12:47.350
is what is the
backlog for stage A?

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:49.360
If you're familiar with
Google Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:52.340
Google Cloud Pub/Sub will
store a backlog for you.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:55.720
In fact, it will store up to
seven days worth of backlog.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:58.000
This can happen if data is
getting pushed to Pub/Sub

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:00.700
and nobody's pulling
from it, or if data

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:03.310
is getting pushed to
Pub/Sub and you're

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:07.790
pulling from Pub/Sub slower than
the publishers are publishing.

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:10.180
So the first thing we
do for stage A is we

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:11.680
say, OK, our source is Pub/Sub.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:16.310
Let's ask Google Cloud Pub/Sub
what the current backlog is.

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:19.130
So we have an internal API
that gives us the backlog.

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:21.170
It's also available
through Stackdriver.

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:24.200
So you can go to Stackdriver
yourself and look

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:27.530
at the unacked messages
metrics and see

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:29.240
what the signal is
that we're looking at

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:32.510
to know that the backlog
is building up in Pub/Sub

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:37.040
and faster than
we're draining it.

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:39.620
But then remember we have a
streaming shuffle between stage

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:44.690
A and stage B. Stage B is where
we produce the final counts--

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:48.140
the final windowed
counts for these words.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:51.110
So we need to measure the
backlog between stage A and B

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:52.517
as well.

00:13:52.517 --> 00:13:54.100
So if we're pulling
data from Pub/Sub,

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:56.090
and we're keeping
up from Pub/Sub,

00:13:56.090 --> 00:13:58.040
and then we're pushing
data to this next stage

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:03.460
and say, hey, count words, and
the next stage can't keep up,

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:06.290
the backlog for that next
stage will grow as well.

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:10.780
So that also means we don't
have enough workers running

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:12.590
to maintain our pipeline.

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:15.010
So the first backlog
tells us we don't

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:17.567
have enough workers running to
pull from Pub/Sub fast enough.

00:14:17.567 --> 00:14:19.900
The second backlog tells us
we don't have enough workers

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:22.570
internally to do all these
counts we're trying to do.

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:34.150
In this talk, I'm going to make
a few simplifying assumptions

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:37.029
to make the autoscaling
algorithm a little bit simpler.

00:14:37.029 --> 00:14:38.820
So the first assumption
is that every stage

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:40.950
can be sized independently.

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:43.770
That I can look at every
stage in my pipeline,

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:46.200
decide what the appropriate
number of workers

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:48.330
is for that stage,
and then I can

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:51.240
decide, based off of all
that, how many workers I need

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:52.500
for my entire pipeline.

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:55.380
Maybe I take the max of all
those and say that's how many

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:57.420
workers I need.

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:02.070
I'm assuming that the load
is distributed fairly evenly

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:02.950
between workers.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:04.830
So if you remember,
I said that we

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:08.070
put a hash, a prefix
on all the keys.

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:10.050
This actually does
a fairly good job

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:14.060
of distributing load fairly
evenly among all workers.

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:18.196
And finally, in practice,
we apply smoothing.

00:15:18.196 --> 00:15:19.820
So as I'll explain
in a minute, there's

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:21.560
some cost to autoscaling.

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:24.289
So if you have a sudden
spike that lasts a second,

00:15:24.289 --> 00:15:26.080
you don't necessarily
want your autoscaling

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:28.800
to be like, up, down, up,
down, up, down, up, down.

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:32.510
So we apply a smoothing window,
which currently is in practice,

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:34.820
I believe a two minute
smoothing window.

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:41.190
So we have some sort of smoother
autoscaling up and down.

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:44.870
So this means that autoscaling
works fairly well when you have

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:48.890
continuous variations in load.

00:15:48.890 --> 00:15:53.450
If your problem is my
load is usually 10 QPS,

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:56.550
and then once an hour
it spikes to a million

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:59.150
QPS for five
seconds, autoscaling

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:03.200
is going to take some time to
go up and down for that spike.

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:12.730
So how do we use these
signals to actually decide

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:15.380
we need to autoscale up?

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:17.720
Well, when the backlog
is high or when

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:21.260
we see the backlog is growing,
which is a sure sign that we

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:25.580
don't have enough resources
to run this pipeline,

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:27.650
we start generating
size recommendations.

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:30.920
And we have the Dataflow
service and our backend

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:34.940
has a loop that is constantly
generating size recommendations

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:37.020
for our pipeline.

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:39.320
So what is a size
recommendation?

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:42.980
Well, first of all, we
measure the input rate.

00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:44.470
And if you remember,
the input rate

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:47.200
is the rate we're
currently processing

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:51.700
at plus the rate at which
the backlog for the pipeline

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:52.910
is increasing.

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:56.045
And that's the estimate of
how fast the data is really

00:16:56.045 --> 00:16:56.545
arriving.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:00.720
After we've measured
the input rate,

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:03.280
it turns out we're
not quite done.

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:05.520
Because if my
backlog is currently

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:10.050
1 terabyte, and I upscale until
I can handle the current input

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:13.566
rate of data, my backlog will
just stay frozen at 1 terabyte.

00:17:13.566 --> 00:17:14.940
Because I now have
enough workers

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:17.880
to handle the data
as it's coming in,

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:21.490
but not enough workers
to drain that backlog.

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:24.030
So we then have to
add to that, how many

00:17:24.030 --> 00:17:27.150
workers do I need to
consume that backlog quickly

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:30.130
while maintaining the
current input rate?

00:17:30.130 --> 00:17:33.670
Then eventually we
will drain that backlog

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:35.675
and be at a backlog of
zero or close to zero.

00:17:35.675 --> 00:17:38.650
And at that point, all
we need is enough workers

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:42.210
to keep up with the input rate.

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:45.070
And as I just mentioned, we
average this recommendation

00:17:45.070 --> 00:17:47.780
over a sliding
two minute window.

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:50.080
So we have a little
bit of smoothing

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:51.731
on these recommendations.

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:57.480
So what do we do when the
estimated backlog is low?

00:17:57.480 --> 00:17:59.860
So how do we decide that
we have too many workers

00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:03.810
and we can now
downscale this job?

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:07.640
Well, I'll divide it
into the current world

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:11.350
and the new streaming engine
world, which is actually

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:13.750
in beta and can be tried now.

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:18.460
The current world takes
a new recommendation,

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:22.540
but actually has to be very
conservative in downscaling.

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:25.780
And the reason why
is, if you remember,

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:29.500
the current architecture
attaches persistent disks

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:31.090
to every worker.

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:37.670
And the work key ranges are
tied to these persistent disks.

00:18:37.670 --> 00:18:40.790
Empirically, we found that
if you get an imbalance--

00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:43.000
so you start having
like 10 persistent disks

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:46.080
on this worker, and 2 persistent
disks on this worker--

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:48.650
it actually makes
performance worse,

00:18:48.650 --> 00:18:50.580
because the workers
with a very small number

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:52.204
of persistent disks
become a bottleneck

00:18:52.204 --> 00:18:54.060
for all the other workers.

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:56.000
So on the persistent
disk architecture,

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:58.880
we attempt to only downscale
if we can make sure

00:18:58.880 --> 00:19:00.590
that all the workers
have an even number

00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:04.800
of persistent disks, which often
means that if we can downscale

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:08.390
by one VM, we might
hold off and say

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:10.250
that'll create an imbalance.

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:14.339
We're going to wait until we can
downscale by enough VMs to even

00:19:14.339 --> 00:19:15.380
out the persistent disks.

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:18.470
And only then will we downscale.

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:21.830
On streaming engine,
our new architecture,

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:24.320
there are no persistent
disks attached to the VMs.

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:25.820
The VMs are stateless.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.580
So we can download continuously.

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:31.790
So we could just
monitor the CPU.

00:19:31.790 --> 00:19:37.230
We target downscaling a
CPU utilization of 80%.

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:39.290
So you do not want
to target 100% CPU

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:41.120
utilization, because
that does not

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.580
give you enough wiggle room.

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:46.220
So often you might
have small spikes

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:48.840
that are not high enough
to trigger upscaling

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:50.570
or not long enough
to trigger upscaling.

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:54.260
You want to actually have
enough wiggle room handle

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:55.840
small variations in input.

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:01.040
So this is what our
worker instance looks

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:04.970
like in the current world.

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:07.340
So this is a GCVM.

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:09.950
We run the SDK worker.

00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:13.830
This is the Dataflow or a Beam
code that you have written.

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:16.250
So this is a Java or
a Python process that

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:20.060
runs the dataflow pipeline.

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:24.920
There is another process
that runs on this VM that

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:27.080
is our streaming engine worker.

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:28.550
This is the process
that knows how

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:32.450
to interpret the
code you've written

00:20:32.450 --> 00:20:39.640
and turn it into persistent
shuffle and so on.

00:20:39.640 --> 00:20:44.432
And then there are a number
of persistent disks attached

00:20:44.432 --> 00:20:45.140
to these workers.

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:51.730
This is the world
we're moving to.

00:20:51.730 --> 00:20:55.330
So the new world,
all of the GC workers

00:20:55.330 --> 00:20:56.950
are completely stateless.

00:20:56.950 --> 00:20:59.620
There is no persistent
disk attached

00:20:59.620 --> 00:21:03.970
to them, which means we can
just add more or tear them down

00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:04.930
as we want.

00:21:04.930 --> 00:21:07.570
There is less cost associated.

00:21:07.570 --> 00:21:11.170
It's less complex, so we don't
have to detach disks, move them

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:12.640
to another VM and attach them.

00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:18.790
And we also do no longer
have this restriction

00:21:18.790 --> 00:21:20.860
of trying to keep an
even number of disks

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:24.880
attached to each worker, because
there are no disks to attach.

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:26.830
In this world, the
Dataflow service

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:29.020
and the streaming engine
service both in the cloud.

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:32.640
And all the state storage and
all the timers and so on also

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:34.240
run in the cloud.

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.190
Which, as a side
note, something I

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:38.740
forgot to mention earlier when
I was talking about streaming

00:21:38.740 --> 00:21:41.710
signals, we actually have
one more streaming signal.

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:44.770
The way windowing
works in Dataflow

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:47.710
is under the covers our
streaming engine has

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:51.070
a notion of timers, where for
every key we're processing,

00:21:51.070 --> 00:21:53.950
it can set timers at the
end of the current window.

00:21:53.950 --> 00:21:56.755
And when the window expires,
all these timers fire.

00:21:56.755 --> 00:21:58.755
And when the timers fire,
we say, OK, the window

00:21:58.755 --> 00:22:00.290
is done for this key.

00:22:00.290 --> 00:22:02.560
Process the counter
and spit it out.

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:04.910
We can measure timer backlog.

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:07.880
So we can measure that a lot
of timers are about to fire.

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:11.810
What this means practically
is our autoscaling

00:22:11.810 --> 00:22:14.330
can see the future in a way.

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:18.560
We can detect that, hey, their
input rate is still steady,

00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:20.900
but we're about to hit
the end of a window,

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:22.670
and all of these timers
are about to fire.

00:22:22.670 --> 00:22:24.879
We better start
autoscaling now so we

00:22:24.879 --> 00:22:27.170
don't hit the end of the
window and then suddenly start

00:22:27.170 --> 00:22:31.250
thrashing, and wait two minutes,
and then start autoscaling up.

00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:35.870
So this allows window processing
to autoscale much more smoothly

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:38.530
by being able to use timers
as one of our signals.

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:46.420
And as I said, no persistent
disk, key ranges are fluid.

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:47.820
This is in beta.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:51.300
So you can try this
today on your pipeline.

00:22:51.300 --> 00:22:56.110
It is currently only launched in
beta right now in two regions.

00:22:56.110 --> 00:23:01.879
So right now, US central one
and Europe West one I believe

00:23:01.879 --> 00:23:03.920
are the two regions where
we've launched this in.

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:06.150
But in one of those
two regions, you

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:08.700
can pass in this
experimental flag,

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:12.120
and it will run on
the new beta engine.

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:14.160
And if you have any issues
running this engine,

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:15.810
please let us know immediately.

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:19.200
This is why things are on
beta so we could shake out

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:21.610
all problems with the service
before we launch it to GA.

00:23:25.210 --> 00:23:27.460
So there's some
cost to resizing.

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:30.520
So in the persistent
disk world, the cost is--

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:33.660
there's latency detaching
and moving disks around

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:35.500
and reattaching them.

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:39.160
There's some startup latency
for a virtual machine.

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:43.282
We have to pause work while
the rebalance is in process.

00:23:43.282 --> 00:23:43.990
You had a worker.

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:46.760
You have to move some work from
another work to the new one.

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:50.110
And for all these reasons,
after a resizing event,

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:53.030
you need a stabilization window.

00:23:53.030 --> 00:23:55.090
So what you don't want
is a hysteresis problem

00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:57.970
where I resize and
move work around,

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:01.120
and that temporarily slows
things down slightly,

00:24:01.120 --> 00:24:04.750
and that makes me think that I'm
falling more behind so I scale

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:06.220
up more and scale up more.

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:10.210
You can have a positive feedback
loop that goes out of control.

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:12.010
So after a resizing
event, instead,

00:24:12.010 --> 00:24:14.290
we introduce a
stabilization period

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:18.070
to make sure the system can
quiet down after a resize

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:22.720
before we examine it again
and decide to resize again.

00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:25.060
The hope is that our initial
estimate on the resize

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:28.540
was accurate so we won't
have to resize again quickly.

00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:31.250
But if we do, we need
to wait slightly longer

00:24:31.250 --> 00:24:32.020
before doing so.

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:39.900
And finally, low CPU
with high backlog.

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:42.810
There are cases in which
the backlog keeps going up.

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:45.840
We start adding more
workers, adding more workers.

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:48.720
And then we look at the workers
and say all these worker,

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:52.260
none of these VMs are
using more than 10% of CPU.

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:55.750
All this autoscaling
up is not helping.

00:24:55.750 --> 00:25:00.020
And the reason why could
be that maybe your pipeline

00:25:00.020 --> 00:25:03.100
is talking to external service
and you're being throttled.

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:06.000
So adding more workers
is not helping matters.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:08.440
It might be making matters
worse because you're just

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:10.630
hammering that external
service even more.

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:12.670
And it's going to start
throttling you more.

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:14.650
Could also be due to
a bug in a pipeline.

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:17.130
Maybe your pipeline is
throwing an exception.

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:20.180
And so that's why it's
not making progress.

00:25:20.180 --> 00:25:22.730
We're not helping matters
by adding more workers.

00:25:22.730 --> 00:25:25.870
So more workers can throw
exceptions in parallel.

00:25:25.870 --> 00:25:28.090
So for this reason, we
actually have a CPU cap.

00:25:28.090 --> 00:25:31.085
If the CPU is too low,
we stop upscaling.

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:36.720
So I mentioned Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:39.750
So Cloud Pub/Sub is
natively supported

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:42.450
by our streaming engine.

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:43.980
And there is an API--

00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:45.810
currently an
internal API that we

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:48.900
use to measure the
backlog and the watermark

00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:49.830
for Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:53.424
Although this backlog API is
also available via Stackdriver.

00:25:53.424 --> 00:25:54.920
You can go to
Stackdriver and add

00:25:54.920 --> 00:25:57.390
an [INAUDIBLE] enact metric
to monitor the backlog

00:25:57.390 --> 00:25:58.440
for your Pub/Sub topic.

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:04.760
The Beam SDK also
supports custom sources.

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:08.200
So if you have a
custom source, you

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:11.890
have to implement these
two methods, which

00:26:11.890 --> 00:26:16.810
allow you to report what the
current backlog of your custom

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:18.100
source is.

00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:20.995
And we can use that
to estimate backlog

00:26:20.995 --> 00:26:23.560
on the source and upscale.

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:25.660
If you have a source that
doesn't report those,

00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:28.390
upscaling will still happen
based off our internal backlog

00:26:28.390 --> 00:26:31.210
measurement, but it will not
work as well because we cannot

00:26:31.210 --> 00:26:35.320
measure what the actual
backlog on the source is.

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:38.440
And Pub/Sub, which
I already mentioned,

00:26:38.440 --> 00:26:40.540
our Kafka source and
our Kinesis source

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:41.900
support this out of the box.

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:43.939
So if you use either
Kafka or Kinesis,

00:26:43.939 --> 00:26:44.980
backlog will be measured.

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:51.390
So in conclusion, I talked
about our primary signals

00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:56.880
for autoscaling, a bit
about how our Java SDK is

00:26:56.880 --> 00:26:59.520
supported for autoscaling.

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:02.130
Python streaming, which
is currently in beta,

00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:05.870
will be supporting
autoscaling very, very soon.

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:08.820
And the new streaming engine,
which is currently in beta,

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:11.130
will take our
existing autoscaling

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:15.270
and make it work
that much better.

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:18.570
And now I'm delighted
to introduce

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:21.870
Roy Bryant from Scotiabank.

00:27:21.870 --> 00:27:24.060
Scotiabank has been
one of the leaders

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:27.060
in the financial services
industry of moving

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:29.850
their processing over to
Google Cloud Platform,

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:32.400
especially this type
of data processing.

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:34.350
And Roy is here to
tell us a little bit

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:37.674
about what they've done
and how they've done it.

00:27:37.674 --> 00:27:40.536
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:42.921 --> 00:27:45.520
ROY BRYANT: Thanks, Reuven.

00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:48.020
Yeah, so I'm going to bring it
up a little bit higher level.

00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:53.000
Reuven's been talking about the
details of how Dataflow works.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:55.460
And I'm just going to
talk a bit about the use

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:56.390
cases we're using.

00:27:56.390 --> 00:27:58.590
We're trying to
transform the bank.

00:27:58.590 --> 00:28:01.550
Pub/Sub and Dataflow are
foundational to that.

00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:04.420
So I just want to talk a
bit about what we're doing.

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:06.710
A little bit about
Scotiabank, in case you

00:28:06.710 --> 00:28:08.580
don't know anything about it.

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:10.200
The bank's been
around a long time.

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:13.910
It's in the top 40 or 25
largest banks in the world,

00:28:13.910 --> 00:28:15.560
depending on how
you measure that.

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:17.420
It's really international.

00:28:17.420 --> 00:28:21.110
It was founded in
Nova Scotia long ago,

00:28:21.110 --> 00:28:24.410
and doing trade that
came into the port.

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:27.400
So more than half the
business is outside Canada.

00:28:27.400 --> 00:28:30.710
There is business
in 55 countries,

00:28:30.710 --> 00:28:32.750
23 million customers,
which is getting

00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:35.790
close to the
population of Canada.

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:39.320
And so we've got
a few challenges

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:40.880
in the infrastructure.

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:42.920
Part of it is it's been
around a long time.

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:47.480
So we have legacy backend
systems more than 50 years old.

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:51.140
We're doing lots of digital
banking and transformation,

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:54.150
online stuff in many
regions, many countries.

00:28:54.150 --> 00:28:56.190
So we want to go fast there.

00:28:56.190 --> 00:28:57.960
We've also done a
lot of acquisitions.

00:28:57.960 --> 00:29:02.570
So over one 10-year period,
we acquired 30 banks,

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:04.187
each with two data centers.

00:29:04.187 --> 00:29:05.520
So we have lots of data centers.

00:29:05.520 --> 00:29:07.311
And sort of this effort
to consolidate that

00:29:07.311 --> 00:29:08.840
and rationalize the stacks.

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:14.980
So we've got some complexity.

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:19.200
So the trust and
the reputation is

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:21.300
foundational to
the business, as it

00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:24.690
is with the financial services
across financial services.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:27.490
And how we handle
PII is key to that.

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:30.270
So big step is moving
the PII to the cloud

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:32.830
and making sure
that's entirely safe.

00:29:32.830 --> 00:29:35.010
And we've got a lot of
stakeholders, security

00:29:35.010 --> 00:29:39.060
and operations, as every
large organization does.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:40.710
We also have, in
addition to legal,

00:29:40.710 --> 00:29:44.250
we've got internal and external
auditing, we've got regulators,

00:29:44.250 --> 00:29:45.650
and we're in 55 countries.

00:29:45.650 --> 00:29:47.760
So we've got 55 regulators.

00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:53.790
And there's a tension about
the velocity for the digital.

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:56.895
We want to go fast and
iterate and get features out

00:29:56.895 --> 00:29:59.220
to customers moving agile.

00:29:59.220 --> 00:30:02.730
And then we also have a lot of
backend infrastructure teams

00:30:02.730 --> 00:30:07.140
that we care a lot about
stability and reliability

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:08.050
and everything.

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:09.760
We actually want both places.

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:12.300
We want the digital stuff
to be stable and reliable,

00:30:12.300 --> 00:30:15.360
and we want to bring more
velocity to the core banking

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:16.240
systems.

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:19.770
So we're trying to
do that using cloud.

00:30:19.770 --> 00:30:22.470
When we think about cloud,
we're thinking about it

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:25.810
more as a method rather
than a destination.

00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:31.290
So we conflate cloud as
moving it to cloud along

00:30:31.290 --> 00:30:36.810
with DevOps and CICD and
test-driven development and all

00:30:36.810 --> 00:30:37.380
those pieces.

00:30:39.950 --> 00:30:43.030
So we've got two
production systems that

00:30:43.030 --> 00:30:46.150
are using Pub/Sub and Dataflow.

00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:48.700
First one I'll talk about
is our log streaming.

00:30:48.700 --> 00:30:52.150
So we've got pulling in
structured and unstructured

00:30:52.150 --> 00:30:55.810
data and logs from Stackdriver
from many different sources,

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:58.450
from all of the
projects running.

00:30:58.450 --> 00:31:02.020
And then we publish
those to Pub/Sub.

00:31:02.020 --> 00:31:06.370
And then use Dataflow to
route those to secure SEC Ops

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:10.480
and persist them for regulatory
purposes, feed it back to Ops,

00:31:10.480 --> 00:31:12.170
all that kind of thing.

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:15.640
And for this, we
found it works--

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:19.990
Pub/Sub and Dataflow work
well, and they're performant,

00:31:19.990 --> 00:31:22.000
and they give us the
performance we need.

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:26.712
And we don't really see any
issue with that going forward.

00:31:26.712 --> 00:31:27.670
So we're happy with it.

00:31:27.670 --> 00:31:30.790
This isn't really
transformational.

00:31:30.790 --> 00:31:33.152
The second use case I
think is more interesting

00:31:33.152 --> 00:31:34.610
because it's the
foundation for how

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:37.690
we're transforming the bank.

00:31:37.690 --> 00:31:40.900
So the problem we're
trying to solve

00:31:40.900 --> 00:31:45.190
is we've got a lot of
different backend systems

00:31:45.190 --> 00:31:47.170
with many data sources.

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:50.380
So in Canadian banking, we've
got over 2,000 production

00:31:50.380 --> 00:31:53.800
databases, all with data
in different formats

00:31:53.800 --> 00:31:56.620
and different
representations, no agreement

00:31:56.620 --> 00:31:59.750
on what a customer is and what
a first name of a customer is,

00:31:59.750 --> 00:32:01.520
and what an account
is and so forth.

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:04.170
So we're trying to
rationalize that.

00:32:04.170 --> 00:32:05.660
So it's the 2,000 in Canada.

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:08.120
And we're in 55 countries.

00:32:08.120 --> 00:32:10.750
We're not going to in-place
just start refactoring

00:32:10.750 --> 00:32:11.560
all our databases.

00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:13.250
It's just not going to work.

00:32:13.250 --> 00:32:16.210
So what we're doing
is we're using cloud

00:32:16.210 --> 00:32:23.980
as an opportunity to restructure
the way we do all this.

00:32:23.980 --> 00:32:27.070
So on ingestion, we're
transforming the data

00:32:27.070 --> 00:32:28.330
into a standard format.

00:32:28.330 --> 00:32:31.000
So we've got an enterprise data
model and a business glossary

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:33.680
that you can define what
exactly is a customer.

00:32:33.680 --> 00:32:35.200
What is a customer's first name?

00:32:35.200 --> 00:32:37.810
And then we map from
local schemas in all

00:32:37.810 --> 00:32:39.970
the different data sources.

00:32:39.970 --> 00:32:40.840
We bring that in.

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:44.837
We map it to the
standard schema,

00:32:44.837 --> 00:32:46.420
say this is the
customer's first name.

00:32:46.420 --> 00:32:48.100
This is an account
number, all that.

00:32:48.100 --> 00:32:49.600
Bring the data all
into one place.

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:51.950
That does a bunch of things.

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:57.010
One is that it opens the
door for doing analytics.

00:32:57.010 --> 00:32:59.620
And you can do analytics
across many data

00:32:59.620 --> 00:33:05.000
sources, all the performance,
and so forth from that.

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:08.500
The other thing is that it puts
a cache of the data in cloud.

00:33:08.500 --> 00:33:12.640
So we do a bunch of
Canadian banking.

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:14.680
And you're using your
handset and you're

00:33:14.680 --> 00:33:16.420
doing online banking.

00:33:16.420 --> 00:33:18.160
And then we've got
backends in Toronto.

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:20.230
We've got backends in Chile.

00:33:20.230 --> 00:33:21.280
Everything's far apart.

00:33:21.280 --> 00:33:23.080
So what we're doing
is we're taking

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:25.810
a cache of things in
cloud so we can put

00:33:25.810 --> 00:33:27.760
the compute close to the data.

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:32.970
So we're calling
this the event layer.

00:33:32.970 --> 00:33:35.830
This event layer,
there's two pieces

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:40.600
that Pub/Sub and
Dataflow are key in.

00:33:40.600 --> 00:33:46.260
One is the ingesting the
data into the common format.

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:49.300
So for here, we're streaming
from backend systems.

00:33:49.300 --> 00:33:53.710
And it's MQ, COBOL copybook,
whatever, down there.

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:55.180
Transform that.

00:33:55.180 --> 00:33:56.860
Stream that up into cloud.

00:33:56.860 --> 00:33:58.370
It comes into Pub/Sub.

00:33:58.370 --> 00:34:00.520
And then we use Dataflow.

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:02.360
So on ingestion,
we're transforming it

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:03.610
to the standard schema.

00:34:03.610 --> 00:34:11.020
We're also using DLP and
KMS in tokenizing the data.

00:34:11.020 --> 00:34:12.190
So we've got it in flight.

00:34:12.190 --> 00:34:13.179
Comes in.

00:34:13.179 --> 00:34:18.560
And then in Dataflow, call DLP
on the fly as it flows through.

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:19.420
Tokenize that.

00:34:19.420 --> 00:34:20.980
Land it in Spanner.

00:34:20.980 --> 00:34:24.199
Now it's all
encrypted, safe there.

00:34:24.199 --> 00:34:27.659
And then we just lock down
re-identification separately.

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:36.030
So there's the standard
format, getting the cache.

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:38.650
The other piece is
moving to event driven.

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:40.929
So we're taking
events that can be

00:34:40.929 --> 00:34:45.179
infrastructure events or
security or operations,

00:34:45.179 --> 00:34:47.639
also just changes
in backend data.

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:50.670
We're detecting that,
raising an event for that.

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:52.679
Or a transaction
comes in and then

00:34:52.679 --> 00:34:54.600
we're persisting
the transactions.

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:56.639
So now instead of
the application

00:34:56.639 --> 00:35:01.560
needing to figure out how to
talk to many backend systems

00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:04.800
and having the business
logic in the application

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:06.880
and in the backend
systems, and the APIs,

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:09.390
and APIs call other APIs,
and it is really hard

00:35:09.390 --> 00:35:11.250
to tell what the
business logic is.

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:14.640
So instead, about changing
it to the event flow.

00:35:14.640 --> 00:35:16.890
And then that gives us
all the data in the cloud.

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:19.030
And then when we
start looking forward,

00:35:19.030 --> 00:35:21.750
we can see what are the
changes in the backend system.

00:35:21.750 --> 00:35:23.880
So if we can understand
the data and we build up

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:26.790
a history of exactly how
the data changes on events,

00:35:26.790 --> 00:35:29.310
we can start reversing
out and making sure

00:35:29.310 --> 00:35:31.940
that we've got the
business logic right.

00:35:31.940 --> 00:35:35.190
So if we're trying to replace a
backend system, we can run it.

00:35:35.190 --> 00:35:39.660
We've got some code that we
assert works the same way.

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:42.390
You can run it through all
the last year's transactions

00:35:42.390 --> 00:35:44.370
and see if you get the
same answer every time.

00:35:52.200 --> 00:35:58.000
And then for the APIs, we don't
want all these layered APIs

00:35:58.000 --> 00:35:59.230
on the backend systems.

00:35:59.230 --> 00:36:03.820
So what we do is we bring the
data into the event layer.

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:05.560
So applications can
either subscribe

00:36:05.560 --> 00:36:08.710
to the stream of events and
see transactions that way,

00:36:08.710 --> 00:36:11.890
or could query the storage.

00:36:11.890 --> 00:36:14.380
Or we also expose
it through APIs.

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:17.170
So we have a data
as a service API

00:36:17.170 --> 00:36:22.900
that lets you
access API driven--

00:36:22.900 --> 00:36:24.605
do the queries against the data.

00:36:24.605 --> 00:36:26.230
And then we have a
bank as an API level

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:28.360
above that with bank concepts.

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:31.030
You can say, what
are the accounts

00:36:31.030 --> 00:36:32.620
that this customer has?

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:35.650
And then it will use the data
APIs, it can do the queries.

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:39.840
Give a structured
way to get at that.

00:36:42.410 --> 00:36:45.780
So what we've accomplished.

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:48.878
So we have these couple
of things in production.

00:36:56.470 --> 00:36:59.560
And as I said,
the transformation

00:36:59.560 --> 00:37:03.760
to standardized data
and event driven

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:05.770
is really foundational
to our strategy

00:37:05.770 --> 00:37:07.690
for transforming the bank.

00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:10.324
And then Pub/Sub
and Dataflow are

00:37:10.324 --> 00:37:11.740
foundational to
the implementation

00:37:11.740 --> 00:37:14.020
of that strategy.

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:18.970
And we've been really happy
with the performance, the cost,

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:20.200
reliability of the systems.

00:37:20.200 --> 00:37:23.650
They've all worked really
well in production.

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:26.904
The other factor we considered
in choosing Google to do this

00:37:26.904 --> 00:37:28.570
is there's a really
strong relationship,

00:37:28.570 --> 00:37:29.778
a collaborative relationship.

00:37:29.778 --> 00:37:32.020
And we got a lot of
engineering support.

00:37:32.020 --> 00:37:33.940
So it's gone really well.

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:38.120
Thank you.

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:38.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:38.720 --> 00:37:42.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

